  "weakest_point": "The nineteenth primitive boundary adds the direct return-to-zero req/ack handshake branch, but the active primitive set still has no standalone arbitration-policy primitive. A fixed-priority arbiter gate can cover one concrete ready/valid grant-selection policy while keeping round-robin/tree arbitration, all N/DW settings, EnDataPort=0 behavior, fairness properties, alert/esc differential signaling, clock-gating correctness, raw full-state equality, and zero-initialized GPU correctness outside the claim.",
  "why_this_target": [
    "the nineteenth primitive review accepted prim_sync_reqack and leaves arbitration-policy primitives outside the active claim",
    "prim_arbiter_fixed is a single upstream OpenTitan primitive with a concrete fixed-priority ready/valid contract",
    "choosing N=4, DW=16, and EnDataPort=1 exercises the non-degenerate tree and data path without moving into broader arbiter families",
    "it is narrower than alert/esc differential signaling and full IP blocks",
    "it avoids clock-special behavior in prim_clock_gating while adding more behavioral value than a standalone security-anchor buffer wrapper"
  ],
  "rejected_for_now": [
    {
      "target": "prim_sec_anchor_buf",
      "reason": "standalone wrapper is only 21 lines and has already appeared in active life-cycle and MuBi dependency paths; it adds less behavior than a fixed-priority arbiter gate"
